[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m distinguishes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Axis enumeration (from the source, not from guesses)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Public entry points** — the full set, from `include/lib.h`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | entry point | kind |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `md5_digest(const tflac_md5*, tflac_u8[16])` | the **only** symbol, and it is the lowest-level one — there is no convenience/one-shot wrapper layered over anything, so "test the low-level API too" is satisfied by testing it directly |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Runtime options / modes / flags:** none. `grep -nE 'if\|switch\|#if'` over the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m library returns no hits, so there is no flag the API can set and no branch to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m toggle. No option axis exists.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Input shapes the code special-cases:** the signature is fully fixed — one
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 16-byte struct in, one 16-byte buffer out. There is no count, no width, no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m element type, no format selector, and no empty/one/many axis. What *is*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m value-dependent is the 16 independent byte-extraction expressions
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `(tflac_u8)(field >> {0,8,16,24})` for `field ∈ {a,b,c,d}`. So the shapes that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the code can distinguish are:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **F — field identity** (which of the 4 words a byte came from): struct offsets 0/4/8/12
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **P — byte position within a word** (which shift): 0/8/16/24
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **V — value pattern**: zero, all-ones, single-bit, high-bit-set, per-byte-distinct, random
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **A — `out` pointer alignment**: 4-aligned vs offset 1/2/3 (legal: `out` is `uint8_t*`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **M — `m` pointer alignment**: 4-aligned (natural) vs misaligned
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **O — `m`/`out` overlap**: disjoint, or aliased at various offsets. **Neither
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   parameter is `restrict`-qualified**, so overlap is a legal call. The C reads
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `m->a`, `m->b`, `m->c`, `m->d` *interleaved with* the stores, so with overlap
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the later reads observe bytes the earlier stores already wrote. This is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   one place where a naive Rust translation can legitimately diverge (a Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `&tflac_md5` shared reference permits LLVM to hoist all four loads above the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stores), so it is enumerated as a first-class axis rather than skipped.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **B — buffer extent**: exactly 16 bytes, larger than 16 (must leave the tail
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   untouched), and 16 bytes ending flush against an unmapped guard page (must
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   not read or write a 17th byte)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **S — call sequencing**: single call, repeated calls into one buffer, one
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   struct into two buffers (must be stateless)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Endianness: the shift expressions hard-code little-endian output independent of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m host byte order; the test host is x86-64 LE. Both implementations are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-order-explicit (`>> 8*k` vs `to_le_bytes`), so there is no host-dependent
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m row that can be exercised here; noted for completeness.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Rows (cross-product, pruned to what the C distinguishes)
