[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Divergence 1 — row C12, misaligned `m` (found by C12)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust formed a `&tflac_md5` shared reference from the raw pointer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`let m = unsafe { &*m };`). With the struct placed at byte offset 1 of a heap
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffer, that aborted:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m misaligned pointer dereference: address must be a multiple of 0x4 but is 0x7f83ec0010b1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whereas the C performs an ordinary (unaligned-capable) x86-64 dword load and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m returns the correct bytes. Fixed by removing the reference and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `&mut [u8]` slice entirely and working through raw pointers at byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m granularity, where alignment is vacuous.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Divergence 2 — rows C15–C20, read/store interleaving under aliasing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Neither C parameter is `restrict`-qualified, so `out` may overlap `*m`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Disassembling the actual C `.so` (`CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` is empty, so no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m optimization) shows the field is **reloaded from memory before each of the 16
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m single-byte stores**:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mov -0x8(%rbp),%rax ; mov (%rax),%eax ; mov %dl,(%rax)     ; out[0] = (u8)a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mov -0x8(%rbp),%rax ; mov (%rax),%eax ; shr $0x8,%eax ; ... ; out[1] = (u8)(a>>8)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust instead read each field once and wrote 4 bytes at a time via
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `copy_from_slice` (a `memcpy`), so with overlap it produced the *pre-call*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m field values where the C produces the progressively-overwritten ones. Fixed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reading the source byte freshly inside every iteration and emitting exactly one
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m single-byte store per step.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cfg_aliasing_rows_are_discriminating` proves these rows are not vacuous: it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m builds a model of the hoisted-load implementation and asserts the real C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disagrees with it in at least 4 of 6 overlap offsets, and that with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `out = m + 1` the C produces the byte-propagation cascade
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`out[17..33] == [out[16]; 16]`) that only per-byte reloading can produce. Any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m implementation that hoisted its loads would therefore be caught here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Equivalence note
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because `shift == 8*(i%4)` selects byte `i%4` of the field at offset `4*(i/4)`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the byte the C's shift extracts always lives at absolute offset
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `4*(i/4) + (i%4) == i`. Reading only that byte observes the same memory state
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m as the C's full dword load followed by the shift, so the final Rust is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m straight sequential byte copy — which is exactly what the C computes, aliasing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m included.
